## 3. Products and intended use

Korvane takes custody of the container, and in practice of the cargo inside it, for a few hours up to a few days. We do not know the full composition of most loads; we do know the temperature regime the shipper has asked for. For that reason we classify cargo into six product classes by temperature requirement, not by commodity. The class is entered in the booking in Tidewalk (field "TempClass") and is printed on the yard card of every container. If the shipper's instruction and the class table below disagree, the stricter limit applies until the customer has confirmed the instruction in writing.

| Class | Typical cargo | Set point | Product core limit at gate-in (CCP-1) | Air alarm band |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| CH-1 | Chilled dairy, ready-to-eat chilled products | +1.5 °C | ≤ +4.5 °C | set point +2.0 K |
| CH-2 | Fresh fish and seafood (ice packed) | −0.5 °C | ≤ +2.0 °C | set point +1.5 K |
| CH-3 | Berries and soft fruit | +0.5 °C | ≤ +3.0 °C | set point +2.0 K |
| CH-4 | Citrus and hardy produce | +6.5 °C | between +4.0 °C and +9.0 °C | set point ±2.5 K |
| FZ-1 | Frozen general (meat, bakery, vegetables) | −20.5 °C | ≤ −17.5 °C | set point +3.0 K |
| FZ-2 | Deep-frozen (ice cream, frozen seafood) | −24.0 °C | ≤ −21.0 °C | set point +3.0 K |

The lower limit for CH-4 exists because citrus suffers chilling injury below about +4 °C; the injury is a quality and not a safety matter but our largest citrus customer, Fruttoria Baltica, has made it a contractual limit and so we treat it as one. Humidity is monitored for CH-3 (target relative humidity 90 to 95 per cent) but is not a critical limit.

Intended use of the end product is not affected by our activities. Vulnerable consumers (infants, elderly, immunocompromised) are possible for dairy, ready-to-eat chilled products and seafood; this is why classes CH-1 and CH-2 have the tightest evaluation rules in section 9.

## 4. Process description

The process steps are numbered S1 to S14 and are shown in flow diagram KC-QF-HACCP-001-F1 (attached to the paper master and not reproduced here). Verification of the diagram on the ground was done by walking the process at GDY-1 on 19 June 2024, HAM-2 on 26 June 2024 and RTM-3 on 3 July 2024.

1. **S1 Booking and pre-advice.** The customer or forwarder books a slot in Tidewalk and declares the class. A booking without a class is held at status "TEMPCLASS?" until clarified.
2. **S2 Gate-in.** Documents, seal number and container number are checked against the pre-advice; the container is inspected visually for damage, odour and dripping.
3. **S3 Product temperature check.** Core temperature is measured on sample cartons (CCP-1).
4. **S4 Pre-trip inspection (PTI).** For units that Korvane supplies or services, and for any unit that shows a fault code, the PTI is run (CCP-3).
5. **S5 Plug-in.** The container is connected to a yard pedestal; the time without power is limited (CCP-2).
6. **S6 Monitoring on the yard.** KC-Tag 4 loggers and KC-Hub 200 gateways report to Tidewalk; alarms are answered by the dispatch desk (CCP-5).
7. **S7 Cross-dock transfer.** Pallets are moved between containers through the cold dock (CCP-4).
8. **S8 Stuffing and sealing.** Outbound containers are loaded, sealed and the seal number recorded.
9. **S9 Gate-out and dispatch.** Final temperature and unit status are recorded, documents handed to the driver.
10. **S10 Washing of empty containers** (OPRP-1).
11. **S11 Pest control** (OPRP-2).
12. **S12 Water and ice supply.**
13. **S13 Personal hygiene, glass and hard plastics, pallets** (OPRP-3).
14. **S14 Rejected and returned cargo.**

## 5. Hazard analysis method

Each hazard identified at each step is scored for severity (S, 1 to 5) and likelihood in our operation (L, 1 to 5). The significance score is S × L. A hazard with a score of 12 or more is *significant* and must be controlled by a CCP or an operational prerequisite programme (OPRP); a hazard scoring between 6 and 11 is controlled by the general prerequisite programmes (cleaning, maintenance, pest control, training) and is reviewed at each team meeting; below 6 is recorded only. The scoring scale is in annex 2 of the FSMS manual. The team decides between CCP and OPRP with the decision tree of ISO 22000 clause 8.5.2.4: a control measure is managed as a CCP when a critical limit with a continuous or sufficiently frequent measurement exists and when loss of control cannot be corrected downstream by a later step.

The hazard analysis below was last fully repeated by the team on 11 July 2024, with partial updates on 2 October 2024 (this revision).

### 5.1 Hazard analysis table

| Step | Hazard | Type | S | L | Score | Control |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| S2 Gate-in | Pathogen growth in cargo already warm on arrival (Listeria monocytogenes in CH-1, histamine in CH-2) | B | 5 | 3 | 15 | CCP-1 |
| S2 Gate-in | Broken seal, tampering, undeclared cargo | P/C | 4 | 2 | 8 | OPRP-4 seals |
| S3 Product check | Cross-contamination from reused probe thermometers | B | 3 | 2 | 6 | probe sanitised with 70% isopropanol wipe between containers |
| S4 PTI | Unit unable to hold set point (refrigeration fault) | B | 5 | 3 | 15 | CCP-3 |
| S4 PTI | Refrigerant or oil leak onto cargo | C | 4 | 1 | 4 | recorded only |
| S5 Plug-in | Loss of power for too long, pathogen growth | B | 5 | 4 | 20 | CCP-2 |
| S6 Yard monitoring | Undetected or unanswered temperature excursion | B | 5 | 4 | 20 | CCP-5 |
| S6 Yard monitoring | Diesel exhaust uptake by cargo from idling yard tractors | C | 3 | 2 | 6 | yard rule: no idling within 15 m of the plug rows |
| S7 Cross-dock | Exposure to warm dock air during transfer | B | 4 | 4 | 16 | CCP-4 |
| S7 Cross-dock | Allergen cross-contact (mixed pallets, torn cartons) | C | 4 | 2 | 8 | OPRP-3, torn cartons are re-wrapped and quarantined |
| S7 Cross-dock | Glass or hard plastic from damaged light fittings | P | 4 | 2 | 8 | OPRP-3 |
| S8 Stuffing | Splinters from damaged wooden pallets | P | 3 | 3 | 9 | OPRP-3 pallet inspection |
| S8 Stuffing | Residues of previous cargo or odour in container | C | 3 | 3 | 9 | OPRP-1 |
| S9 Gate-out | Wrong set point left on the unit | B | 5 | 3 | 15 | CCP-3 (final unit check) |
| S10 Washing | Detergent or sanitiser residue in container | C | 4 | 3 | 12 | OPRP-1 |
| S10 Washing | Microbial build-up on floor T-bars and door gaskets | B | 4 | 3 | 12 | OPRP-1 |
| S11 Pests | Rodents, insects entering containers or contaminating pallets | B | 4 | 3 | 12 | OPRP-2 |
| S12 Water | Non-potable wash water | B | 4 | 1 | 4 | recorded only; water from municipal supply, tested twice a year |
| S13 Hygiene | Norovirus from sick staff handling open cartons | B | 4 | 2 | 8 | handbook rule on reporting illness, general PRP |
| S14 Returns | Mixing of rejected and accepted cargo | B | 4 | 2 | 8 | red quarantine rack, general PRP |

The hazard listed under S9 (wrong set point at gate-out) was added in revision 8 after a near miss on 2 November 2022, when a CH-1 container left HAM-2 with a set point of +6.0 °C; it was caught by the receiver and the loss was nil.

